.TH ED 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
ed \- edit text by lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ed
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ed
is the standard text editor.
If the optional argument is given, the file is read into ed's buffer so
that it can be edited.
Ed operates on a copy of any file it is editing; changes made in the copy
have no effect on the file until an explicit write (w) command is given.
The copy is held in process memory on maux.
.PP
Commands to ed have a simple and regular structure: zero or more
addresses followed by a single character command, possibly followed by
parameters to the command. These addresses specify lines in the buffer.
Commands have default addresses, so the addresses can often be omitted.
.PP
While ed is accepting text, it is said to be in input mode.
In this mode no commands are recognized; all input is merely collected.
Input mode is left by typing a period (.) alone on a line.
.PP
.B Addresses.
A decimal number addresses that line, . addresses the current line,
and $ the last line. /pattern/ searches forward, wrapping around.
An address may be followed by +number or -number.
Two addresses separated by a comma form a range.
A semicolon sets the current line before interpreting the second address.
Bare , means 1,$ and bare ; means .,$.
.PP
.B Commands.
In the following list, 'current' means the line most recently affected.
.TP
.B a, i, c
Append after, insert before, or change addressed text.
Accept input through a line containing only a period.
The default is the current line; c can take a range.
.TP
.B d
Delete the addressed lines; default current.
.TP
.B p, n
Print addressed lines, without or with line numbers; default current.
.TP
.B r file
Read a file after the addressed line; default end of buffer.
.TP
.B w file
Write the addressed lines; default the whole buffer.
An omitted file name reuses the remembered name.
Writing replaces the destination contents, including shortening it.
.TP
.B s/pattern/replacement/[g|number][p]
Substitute in the addressed lines; default current.
Without a flag replace the first match; g replaces all matches;
a number selects that occurrence. p prints the result.
In replacement, & means the matched text; backslash quotes a delimiter.
.TP
.B g/pattern/commands, v/pattern/commands
Run commands on lines matching, or not matching, the pattern.
Default range is the whole buffer. A selected line becomes current.
.TP
.B m address, t address
Move or copy the addressed range after address.
.TP
.B j
Join addressed lines without a separator; default current and next.
.TP
.B =
Print a line number; default the last line.
.TP
.B q
Quit. There is no unsaved-change guard in ed.
.PP
.B Regular expressions.
An ordinary character matches itself; . matches any character.
^ at the start and $ at the end anchor a pattern to the line.
[abc] matches a listed character, [a-z] a range, [^abc] a complement.
* repeats the preceding item zero or more times.
The shared matcher also supports \\( ... \\), backreferences \\1 through
\\9, and repetition forms \\{n\\}, \\{n,\\}, \\{n,m\\}, \\+ and \\?.
An empty pattern reuses the last pattern.
This is a basic, not extended, regular-expression syntax.
.PP
For a small new file, type these lines to ed:
.PP
.B a
.br
hello
.br
\&.
.br
.B w hello
.br
.B q
.SH FILES
/usr/man/ED  this page
